# D4.5 — Observational Separation & Quotient

## Derivation status

**Status:** `[DERIVED CORE / MINIMALITY OPEN]`

**Purpose:** Establish the mathematical bridge between the required distinctions of D1 and the representation/quotient structure required for D5 and subsequent Kernel reduction.

The derivation does **not** assume topology, metric structure, probability, vector-space structure, or Markov structure.

The dependency is:

$$
D1
\rightarrow
D2
\rightarrow
D3
\rightarrow
D4
\rightarrow
\boxed{D4.5}
\rightarrow
D5
\rightarrow
D6
\rightarrow
Kernel\ Reduction.
$$

---

# 1. Starting point: Required Distinctions

D1 provides the requirement-relative indistinguishability relation

$$
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}
=
\bigcap_{d\in R_{\mathrm{req}}(Q,\Gamma)}
\sim_d^{Q,\Gamma}.
$$

For fixed \(Q,\Gamma\), two states are equivalent under \(\sim_{\mathrm{req}}\) precisely when **none of the distinctions required by the inquiry and context distinguishes them**.

We therefore take as given:

$$
\boxed{
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}
\text{ is an equivalence relation on }\mathcal S.
}
$$

This is the D1 foundation.

---

# 2. Observable

Let

$$
\mathcal S
$$

be the state space under consideration.

### Definition D4.5.1 — Observable

An **observable** is a mapping

$$
o:\mathcal S\rightarrow X_o
$$

where \(X_o\) is its observation domain.

For the present derivation, an observable is required to be **state-preserving**:

$$
o(s)\text{ evaluates }s
\quad\text{without changing }s.
$$

This is a methodological restriction, not yet a claim about every possible KnowledgeOS operation.

### Important distinction

$$
o
\neq
Obs_o(s).
$$

The first is the observation mapping.

The second is the result.

---

# 3. Observation Domain

### Definition D4.5.2 — Observation Domain

For an observable

$$
o:\mathcal S\rightarrow X_o,
$$

\(X_o\) is its **observation domain**.

At D4.5 we assume only:

$$
X_o\neq\varnothing
$$

and that equality

$$
=_{X_o}
$$

is available.

No further structure is assumed.

Thus \(X_o\) may be:

* Boolean,
* categorical,
* finite,
* infinite,
* relational,
* structured,
* set-valued,
* numerical,
* temporal,
* probabilistic,

but none of these structures is part of D4.5 merely by being possible.

This is important for Kernel minimality.

---

# 4. Observation Result

### Definition D4.5.3

For

$$
o:\mathcal S\rightarrow X_o,
$$

the observation result for state \(s\) is

$$
\boxed{
Obs_o(s):=o(s).
}
$$

This distinction gives us:

$$
\boxed{
Observable = mapping,
\qquad
Observation = result.
}
$$

---

# 5. Observation Family

Let

$$
\mathcal O=
\{o_\alpha:\mathcal S\rightarrow X_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}.
$$

### Definition D4.5.4 — Observation Family

An **observation family** is a collection of observables indexed by \(A\).

For KnowledgeOS, the relevant family is potentially restricted by inquiry and context:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal O_{\mathrm{adm}}(Q,\Gamma)
\subseteq
\mathcal O.
}
$$

However, we should be careful with the word **authorized**.

Authorization is a governance concept. Therefore I recommend:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal O_{\mathrm{adm}}(Q,\Gamma)
=
\text{observables admissible under the specified semantic/contextual constraints}.
}
$$

Whether authorization is one of those constraints remains a D5/governance question.

---

# 6. Observation Signature

For

$$
\mathcal O=
\{o_\alpha:\mathcal S\to X_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A},
$$

define

$$
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s)
=
(o_\alpha(s))_{\alpha\in A}.
}
$$

Thus

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}:
\mathcal S
\longrightarrow
\prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha.
$$

This is the **observation-generated representation**.

Notice something important:

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}
$$

is not yet the KnowledgeOS state representation.

It is a representation **generated by a specified observation family**.

---

# 7. Separation

### Definition D4.5.5 — Observation-Generated Separation

Two states \(s_1,s_2\in\mathcal S\) are separated by \(\mathcal O\) iff

$$
\boxed{
Separate_{\mathcal O}(s_1,s_2)
\iff
\exists o\in\mathcal O:
o(s_1)\neq_{X_o}o(s_2).
}
$$

Equivalently:

$$
Separate_{\mathcal O}(s_1,s_2)
\iff
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)\neq\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2).
$$

### Proof

By definition,

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)
=
(o_\alpha(s_1))_{\alpha\in A}
$$

and

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2)
=
(o_\alpha(s_2))_{\alpha\in A}.
$$

The product elements differ iff at least one coordinate differs.

Therefore

$$
\boxed{
Separate_{\mathcal O}(s_1,s_2)
\iff
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)\neq\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2).
}
$$

---

# 8. Observational Equivalence

### Definition D4.5.6

Define

$$
\boxed{
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2
\iff
\forall o\in\mathcal O,\;
o(s_1)=_{X_o}o(s_2).
}
$$

Equivalently,

$$
\boxed{
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2
\iff
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)=\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2).
}
$$

Therefore

$$
\boxed{
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho_{\mathcal O})
=
\equiv_{\mathcal O}.
}
$$

Here

$$
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho)
:=
\{(s_1,s_2)\in\mathcal S^2:
\rho(s_1)=\rho(s_2)\}.
$$

### Terminology warning

This must **never** be confused with the KnowledgeOS Kernel:

$$
\boxed{
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho)
\neq
\mathcal K_{\mathrm{Kernel}}.
}
$$

The former is an equivalence relation.

The latter is a candidate foundational mechanism.

---

# 9. Observational Equivalence Is an Equivalence Relation

### Proposition D4.5-P1

$$
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
$$

is an equivalence relation on \(\mathcal S\).

### Proof

**Reflexivity**

For every \(s\),

$$
o(s)=o(s)
$$

for every \(o\in\mathcal O\). Hence

$$
s\equiv_{\mathcal O}s.
$$

**Symmetry**

If

$$
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2,
$$

then

$$
o(s_1)=o(s_2)
$$

for every \(o\), hence

$$
o(s_2)=o(s_1).
$$

Therefore

$$
s_2\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_1.
$$

**Transitivity**

If

$$
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2
$$

and

$$
s_2\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_3,
$$

then for every \(o\),

$$
o(s_1)=o(s_2)=o(s_3).
$$

Therefore

$$
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_3.
$$

Hence:

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}\text{ is an equivalence relation.}
}
$$

This part is mathematically closed.

---

# 10. Requirement-Separating Observation Family

This is the central bridge to D1.

### Definition D4.5.7

An observation family \(\mathcal O\) is **requirement-separating** for \((Q,\Gamma)\) iff

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
}
$$

Interpretation:

> Whenever two states are indistinguishable by all admissible observations, they must also be indistinguishable with respect to every distinction required by \(Q,\Gamma\).

This is exactly the D1 representation adequacy condition.

---

# 11. Requirement-Faithful Representation Theorem

We can now prove the requested theorem.

### Theorem D4.5-T1 — Requirement-Faithful Representation

Let

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}:
\mathcal S\rightarrow
\prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha
$$

be the representation generated by \(\mathcal O\).

Then

$$
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathcal O}
\text{ is requirement-faithful}
\iff
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho_{\mathcal O})
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
}
$$

Since

$$
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho_{\mathcal O})
=
\equiv_{\mathcal O},
$$

we obtain

$$
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathcal O}
\text{ is requirement-faithful}
\iff
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
}
$$

### Proof

By definition,

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}
$$

is requirement-faithful iff

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)
=
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2)
$$

never identifies states that are required to be distinguished.

But

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_1)
=
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s_2)
\iff
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2.
$$

Therefore

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}
\text{ requirement-faithful}
\iff
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
$$

QED.

---

# 12. An important correction: this does NOT prove minimality

This is where I would change the pasted document.

The theorem proves **adequacy**, not minimality.

For example, suppose

$$
\mathcal O=\{o_1,o_2,o_3\}
$$

and \(o_3\) contains exactly the same information as \(o_1\).

Then

$$
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s)
=
(o_1(s),o_2(s),o_3(s))
$$

is requirement-faithful, but \(o_3\) is redundant.

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\text{requirement-faithful}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{minimal}.
}
$$

This is a critical distinction for D5.

---

# 13. Two Different Meanings of Minimality

We need to define them separately.

## 13.1 Inclusion-minimal observation family

An observation family \(\mathcal O\) is **inclusion-minimal requirement-separating** iff

$$
\mathcal O
$$

is requirement-separating and for every

$$
\mathcal O'\subsetneq\mathcal O,
$$

$$
\mathcal O'
$$

is not requirement-separating.

Formally:

$$
\boxed{
Minimal_{\subseteq}(\mathcal O)
\iff
Faithful(\mathcal O)
\land
\forall\mathcal O'\subsetneq\mathcal O:
\neg Faithful(\mathcal O').
}
$$

This is a genuine minimality notion.

---

## 13.2 Cardinality-minimal observation family

If a minimum exists, define

$$
\boxed{
|\mathcal O^\ast|
=
\min
\{
|\mathcal O|:
\mathcal O
\text{ is requirement-separating}
\}.
}
$$

This is stronger.

An inclusion-minimal family need not be cardinality-minimal.

Therefore D4.5 should **not** simply say “minimal observational representation” without specifying which meaning is intended.

---

# 14. The More Fundamental Minimal Representation

There is an even stronger mathematical result.

We already possess

$$
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
$$

Therefore define the quotient

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S_{\mathrm{req}}
=
\mathcal S/
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}.
}
$$

This quotient represents exactly the distinctions required by the inquiry.

Let

$$
q_{\mathrm{req}}:
\mathcal S
\rightarrow
\mathcal S_{\mathrm{req}}
$$

be the quotient map:

$$
q_{\mathrm{req}}(s)
=
[s]_{\sim_{\mathrm{req}}}.
$$

Its representation kernel is exactly

$$
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(q_{\mathrm{req}})
=
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
$$

Hence

$$
\boxed{
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(q_{\mathrm{req}})
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
}
$$

So it is requirement-faithful.

---

# 15. Coarsest Requirement-Faithful Representation

Now we obtain the important minimality result.

### Theorem D4.5-T2 — Coarsest Requirement-Faithful Quotient

Among equivalence relations \(\equiv\) on \(\mathcal S\) satisfying

$$
\equiv
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma},
$$

the relation

$$
\boxed{
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}
}
$$

is the **largest admissible equivalence relation**.

Consequently,

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S/
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}^{Q,\Gamma}
}
$$

is the **coarsest state identification that preserves all required distinctions**.

### Why?

Any representation \(\rho\) that is requirement-faithful satisfies

$$
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho)
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
$$

Thus it may distinguish more states than required, but it cannot identify states that D1 requires us to distinguish.

The quotient by \(\sim_{\mathrm{req}}\) performs the **maximum permissible identification**.

This gives us a mathematically clean concept of minimality.

---

# 16. Critical distinction: minimal quotient vs minimal observable family

We therefore have three different objects:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S
}
$$

original state space,

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S/\sim_{\mathrm{req}}
}
$$

coarsest representation preserving required distinctions,

and

$$
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathcal O}:
\mathcal S\to\prod_{\alpha}X_\alpha
}
$$

a representation generated by a particular observation family.

They must not be conflated.

In particular:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S/\sim_{\mathrm{req}}
\not\equiv
\prod_\alpha X_\alpha
}
$$

in general.

The latter may contain redundant information.

---

# 17. Observational Quotient

### Definition D4.5.8

Given \(\mathcal O\),

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S_{\mathcal O}
=
\mathcal S/\equiv_{\mathcal O}.
}
$$

Its elements are equivalence classes

$$
[s]_{\equiv_{\mathcal O}}.
$$

This construction is mathematically valid because \(\equiv_{\mathcal O}\) is an equivalence relation.

---

# 18. Congruence — Necessary Correction

The pasted formulation defines congruence only for a unary transition

$$
T:\mathcal S\to\mathcal S.
$$

That is sufficient for the unary case, but D5 contains operations such as:

$$
Assert,\ Link,\ Merge,\ Retract,\ldots
$$

which may have different arities.

Therefore D4.5 should use the general definition.

### Definition D4.5.9 — Operational Congruence

Let

$$
o:S^n\rightharpoonup S
$$

be an \(n\)-ary possibly partial operation.

An equivalence relation \(\equiv\) is a congruence for \(o\) iff

$$
s_i\equiv s_i'
\quad
\forall i
$$

implies:

1. **definedness compatibility**, and
2. when defined,

$$
\boxed{
o(s_1,\ldots,s_n)
\equiv
o(s_1',\ldots,s_n').
}
$$

For a total unary operation this reduces to:

$$
s_1\equiv s_2
\Rightarrow
T(s_1)\equiv T(s_2).
$$

This correction is important because D5 already contains **partial operations**.

---

# 19. Why Definedness Matters

Suppose

$$
Merge(K_1,K_2)
$$

is defined, but after replacing \(K_1\) with an observationally equivalent state \(K_1'\), the operation becomes undefined.

Then the quotient cannot faithfully represent the operation.

Thus equality of outputs alone is insufficient.

We require:

$$
\boxed{
\text{equivalent inputs must have compatible operation definedness.}
}
$$

This is the algebraic bridge identified in our previous review of D5.

---

# 20. Induced Quotient Operation

### Definition D4.5.10

If \(\equiv_{\mathcal O}\) is a congruence for \(T\), then define

$$
\boxed{
\bar T:
\mathcal S_{\mathcal O}
\rightarrow
\mathcal S_{\mathcal O}
}
$$

by

$$
\boxed{
\bar T([s])
=
[T(s)].
}
$$

### Well-definedness theorem

If

$$
[s_1]=[s_2],
$$

then

$$
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O}s_2.
$$

By congruence,

$$
T(s_1)\equiv_{\mathcal O}T(s_2).
$$

Therefore

$$
[T(s_1)]
=
[T(s_2)].
$$

Hence \(\bar T\) does not depend on which representative is chosen.

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\text{congruence}
\Rightarrow
\text{well-defined induced operation on the quotient}.
}
$$

This is the exact algebraic point that was missing from the earlier D4/D5 chain.

---

# 21. Representation Minimality

We can now formulate the correct D4.5.11.

### Definition D4.5.11 — Observation-Generated Representation

$$
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathcal O}(s)
=
(o_\alpha(s))_{\alpha\in A}.
}
$$

It satisfies

$$
\ker_{\mathrm{rep}}(\rho_{\mathcal O})
=
\equiv_{\mathcal O}.
$$

The representation is **requirement-faithful** iff

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
}
$$

It is **inclusion-minimal requirement-faithful** iff no proper subfamily of \(\mathcal O\) remains requirement-separating.

It is **cardinality-minimal requirement-faithful** iff it has minimum possible cardinality among requirement-separating families.

These are distinct claims.

---

# 22. The Central D4.5 Diagram

The resulting mathematical structure is:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ccccc}
\mathcal S
&\xrightarrow{\rho_{\mathcal O}}&
\displaystyle\prod_{\alpha\in A}X_\alpha
\\[4pt]
\downarrow q_{\mathcal O}
&&
\\[4pt]
\mathcal S/\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\end{array}}
$$

with

$$
\ker(\rho_{\mathcal O})
=
\equiv_{\mathcal O}.
$$

Requirement faithfulness requires

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
}
$$

The maximal permissible collapse is therefore

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S
\rightarrow
\mathcal S/\sim_{\mathrm{req}}.
}
$$

And an operation \(o\) can descend to the quotient only when

$$
\boxed{
\sim_{\mathrm{req}}
\text{ is a congruence for }o.
}
$$

That is the bridge from **D1 distinction preservation** to **D5 algebraic structure**.

